Native SDDM profile for QSM Direct LXC consoles
=============================================

This opt-in profile runs the complete SDDM daemon and its original greeter
with the container's selected system theme. SDDM/PAM authenticates users,
opens real logind sessions and handles desktop logout. qsm-login and the
custom QSM greeter are not used. With the patched shared platform, the
Proxmox WebRTC API on HTTPS 8006 carries signalling. On official PVE, use
the standalone signalling service, optionally through its TCP 8006 gateway;
see packaging/debian/README.standalone. The SDDM profile itself needs no
additional signalling service or port and does not require patched PVE packages.

Qualification target: unprivileged Ubuntu 24.04, systemd 255, Qt 5 / Plasma
5.27, PVE kernel 7.0.14-14-pve. Other distributions, Qt 6 themes and display
managers such as GDM are not qualified. This first profile offers installed
Wayland sessions; Plasma is integrated with the existing QSM clipboard and
sound agent. X11 remains available through the original QSM login profile.

Foundation
----------
Each console has a dedicated VKMS configfs instance named qsm-VMID-seatN.
The host keeps a stable /dev/qsm-native/VMID-seatN alias and restores the
instances before pve-guests starts. This requires VKMS configfs support in
the host kernel; an older VKMS module with only one global display is not
sufficient. The helper refuses that case. It never passes host VT devices
or alters the physical GPU's seat assignment.

The unprivileged guest publishes seat metadata only for its own VKMS
instances in its private /run/udev database, before logind starts. These
become seatqsm1, seatqsm2, ... with CanGraphical=yes and CanTTY=no. The
system SDDM is replaced as the running display manager by a private SDDM
build with the upstream VT-less support. One daemon manages all seats.

Each seat retains the existing headless sway and permanent input devices
across greeter -> desktop -> logout. Native greeter Wayland sockets relay
SCM_RIGHTS as well as bytes, with isolated runtimes for the shared sddm
account. Desktop connections use a root-owned proxy. PAM grants access only
to that seat's logged-in account; on logout the proxy revokes existing
connections too, stops Plasma graphical services and reaps processes in
closed QSM sessions. SSH sessions are not terminated. The host retains its
PVE-user ownership and reconnect/grace policy. Status failures leave a slot
unavailable rather than declaring it a free login screen.

Plasma's per-user systemd manager and D-Bus are shared across its sessions.
Consequently one account may have only one QSM desktop in the container.
Different accounts can use different seats concurrently. This restriction
is checked in the native PAM session hook before granting display access.

Build the optional SDDM package (inside Ubuntu 24.04)
---------------------------------------------------
Install build dependencies:
  apt-get install build-essential cmake git pkg-config file dpkg-dev \
    qtbase5-dev qtdeclarative5-dev qttools5-dev qttools5-dev-tools \
    libpam0g-dev libsystemd-dev systemd-dev libxcb-xkb-dev libxkbcommon-dev \
    libxkbcommon-x11-dev libxcb1-dev libxau-dev

Get the upstream source and invoke the builder:
  git clone https://github.com/sddm/sddm.git /path/to/sddm
  QSM_SDDM_SOURCE_DIR=/path/to/sddm \
    bash qsm-direct/packaging/lxc/build-qsm-console-sddm-deb.sh

The builder exports the pinned v0.21.0 commit and applies the checked-in
patches; it does not use or modify the checkout's working tree. The patches
contain the upstream VT-less change, native greeter lifecycle/PAM namespace
integration and the per-seat Wayland keyboard backend. See sddm/*.patch for provenance.
Only /opt/qsm-native-sddm and package documentation are installed. Themes,
public units, /etc/pam.d/sddm* and distribution binaries remain owned by the
distribution. qsm-native-profile creates separate qsm-sddm PAM services by
copying the installed distro stacks: authentication/account/password rules
are preserved, pam_loginuid is optional in the container's session stack,
and a session-only access hook is appended. The hook never sees passwords.
Autologin is disabled. Re-run profile configuration after intentionally
changing the distro SDDM PAM policy, before starting new native sessions.

Activate on a PVE node (restarts the selected container)
-------------------------------------------------------
Install matching qsm-pve-direct and qsm-pve-direct-lxc packages. The guest
must already contain a complete Plasma desktop (e.g. kde-plasma-desktop).
Copy qsm-console-sddm_0.21.0+qsm2_amd64.deb to the node, then run:

  qsm-pve-direct-lxc enable VMID --login-manager sddm \
    --sddm-deb /root/qsm-console-sddm_0.21.0+qsm2_amd64.deb \
    --user USER --uid UID --no-gpu --restart

Omit --no-gpu to set up physical GPU passthrough and its userspace drivers.
If passthrough already exists, --no-gpu leaves it available and avoids
reinstalling the drivers. The virtual seat itself needs no physical GPU. User creation/admin/grace
options retain their existing semantics; add --no-admin when appropriate.
Set a local account password with pct exec VMID -- passwd USER. Selection
of this profile is retained on subsequent enable runs and guest upgrades.

Configuration: /etc/qsm-direct/{login-manager,native-seats.json,sddm.conf}.
The native configuration takes its theme from compiled distribution defaults
and the system SDDM configuration at activation. For the original KDE Plasma
login appearance, install its actual theme package (Ubuntu 24.04):
  apt-get install sddm-theme-breeze
Select Current=breeze in [Theme] of /etc/sddm.conf.d/kde_settings.conf, then
rerun qsm-native-profile configure inside the container. Existing explicit
settings in /etc/sddm.conf take precedence over drop-ins. Check the selected
Current in /etc/qsm-direct/sddm.conf. Minimal KDE installations can contain
only Maya, which has a different appearance; QSM does not redraw either theme.
Apply a changed theme when no user desktops are active by restarting
qsm-native-sddm. A restart ends that display manager's current sessions. Other
installed Qt 5 compatible themes can be selected through the same settings.

The original theme's Layout selector now selects real XKB base layouts on
that seat. The private SDDM keyboard backend talks to a restricted root
controller over a Unix socket; ordinary desktop accounts cannot use it.
The selector lists the container's installed XKB registry. Its private QML
component uses a bounded, scrollable menu without modifying the system theme
or the distribution's shared QML files. US, Russian with Shift and German
with AltGr have been qualified through the real WebRTC console.

A new greeter starts with the first valid XKBLAYOUT in /etc/default/keyboard
(or us). Another seat retains its own selection. A new media worker uploads
its initial US map; the host waits for the controller to restore the selected
greeter map before offering browser input. The worker follows compositor
keymap updates so modifiers use the selected layout. The selected map is
confirmed by the controller, rather than changing only the flag in the UI.
On login the outer map returns to US: the nested desktop manages its own
keyboard layouts. Logout or release of an idle login screen starts a fresh
greeter and restores the system default. Custom XKB variants/options and
layout-switching shortcuts are not configured by this selector.

The host creates as many seats as the container's running tty count (1..8).
Changing tty requires stopped-container seat reconfiguration as described
below; it cannot silently allocate another host DRM card to an old seat.

Inspect:
  qsm-pve-direct-lxc status VMID
  pct exec VMID -- loginctl list-seats
  pct exec VMID -- loginctl show-seat seatqsm1 -p CanGraphical -p CanTTY
  pct exec VMID -- journalctl -u qsm-native-sddm -b

Return to the original QSM login profile:
  qsm-pve-direct-lxc enable VMID --login-manager qsm \
    --user USER --uid UID --no-gpu --restart

Disable the graphical console entirely:
  qsm-pve-direct-lxc disable VMID

Device passthrough and VKMS registry are retained on disable/rollback, like
existing GPU passthrough. To remove seats or change their number, first
stop that container, delete only its /dev/qsm-native/VMID-seatN devN entries
from its PVE config, then run:
  /usr/lib/qsm-pve-direct/qsm-native-vkms remove VMID
After starting the container, rerun native enable with --restart to allocate
the current tty count. Never delete another container's configfs instances.
The helper does not unload VKMS or change other displays.
